<h2>The order under challenge</h2>
<p>The Family Court-02, Central District, Tis Hazari, by order dated 4 August 2025 in MT No. 483/2022, directed the petitioner to pay interim maintenance of Rs. 4,500 per month each to his wife and minor daughter. The husband&rsquo;s case in revision was that he had been unemployed since 2023 and that the wife earned around Rs. 1,00,000 per month. The Family Court had fixed the figure by reference to the minimum wages notified for a graduate in Delhi — an approach the High Court found free of irregularity or perversity.</p>

<h2>Two settled propositions, applied together</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Proposition</th><th>Authority applied</th><th>Effect in this case</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Revisional scope against Family Court maintenance orders is confined to perversity, arbitrariness or illegality</td><td>Pyla Mutyalamma v. Pyla Suri Demudu, (2011) 12 SCC 189; Parwez Khan v. Shabnam Ara, 2025:DHC:11152</td><td>The High Court refused to re-appreciate the prima facie assessment</td></tr>
  <tr><td>An able-bodied earner cannot plead his way out of the obligation; undisclosed income invites imputation at minimum wages</td><td>Tasmeer Qureshi v. Asfia Muzaffar, 2025:DHC:9479, drawing on Shamima Farooqui v. Shahid Khan, (2015) 5 SCC 705 and Rajnesh v. Neha</td><td>Rs. 4,500 each, pegged to a graduate&rsquo;s minimum wage, held unexceptionable</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Interim orders do not foreclose modification on proof of changed circumstances</td><td>K.G. v. N.G., 2026 INSC 1215 (no interference with interim figures absent perversity)</td><td>Liberty reserved to seek modification before the Family Court</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The reasoning in brief</h2>
<p>The Court restated that the High Court <em>&ldquo;should not ordinarily interfere with the order of maintenance or interim maintenance passed by the Family Court&rdquo;</em>, and quoted the principle that <em>&ldquo;an able-bodied man cannot be permitted to defeat a claim for maintenance by his wife&rdquo;</em>. Since the Family Court <em>&ldquo;only took into account the minimum wages for a graduate in Delhi&rdquo;</em> — the most conservative basis available — there was nothing perverse to correct. The petition was disposed of with liberty to seek modification, uninfluenced by the High Court&rsquo;s observations.</p>

<h2>Where this fits in Delhi maintenance practice</h2>
<p>The decision is a compact statement of how the two stages of a maintenance proceeding divide their labour. The interim stage runs on affidavits of income and expenditure under the Rajnesh v. Neha framework, a prima facie view, and — where disclosure fails — imputation at the minimum wage for the payer&rsquo;s qualification. The final stage, and the modification jurisdiction, are where evidence of actual unemployment, the claimant&rsquo;s earnings and changed circumstances belong. Attacking an interim imputation in revision, without first building that evidentiary record, rarely succeeds; this order shows why.</p>
<div class="note">Figures in this article are those recorded in the judgment and are case-specific, not benchmarks. Interim maintenance findings are prima facie and do not decide final rights under matrimonial law. The proceeding below was under Section 125 CrPC, instituted before the BNSS came into force; the corresponding provision today is Section 144 BNSS.</div>
